            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

AIR SEALING



Overall, your house is moderately leaky from an air leakage perspective, and I have identified a few locations that can be addressed:



** There were many places on exterior walls where drywall had a hole in it resulting in air leakage. These locations could be fixed and help reduce the air leakage in your home.



** Some windows are leaky around the frames, or do not latch well, and shoud be addressed with a full frame window replacement. See Window upgrade section for more details.



** Around the attic hatch is a small source of air leakage upstairs. This can be sealed with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack".



** The bathroom exhaust fans were both missing, and resulted in air leakage. Bathroom fans can be replaced with energy star rated fans with butterfly style dampers, that seal better than traditional exhaust fan dampers.



** Around the outside edge of both exterior doors. Consider replacing weather stripping as needed, and ensure the latches are working as intended. Alternatively consider a complete door and frame replacement with an ENERGY STAR certified door. See doors section.



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss, but it could result in a larger reduction. This is estimated to save you $190 per year and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.9 GJ. Although the savings aren't huge, the increase in comfort downstairs will be noticeable, for relatively low effort/ expense.



There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

MAIN WALLS



The main exterior walls of your home, for the purposes of our modelling, appear to be of 2x4 construction, at 16 inches on center, with R12 batts in the cavities. For the climate of any location in the NWT, and given the financial and environmental cost of heating northern homes, this is recognized to be insufficient. 



Current building practices suggest a minimum of R30 wall insulation for this climate. I have modelled adding 4 additional inches (R20) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation to 100% of your wall exterioir surfaces. Due to the large amount of labour involved in re-insulating and re-siding a home - and the high cost - this recommendation should only be taken in conjunction with a siding upgrade.



Savings: By adding 4 inches (R20) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls, you could potentially save $ 1700 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 44 GJ, and qualify for up to a $4,000 rebate (if insulating 100% of the surface area) from the GNWT funded, Energy Efficiency Incentive Program administered by AEA. Please note that the rebate amount depends on the percentage of surface area re-insulated, and the R value of insulation added. Additionally, a minimum of 20% of the surface area must be insulated to qualify. If this were something you were interested in doing it would be best to discuss it over the phone in more detail.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate for insulation, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



*** There is an additional, specialized rebate program from Arctic Energy Alliance called  the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, accessble only until March 2024 that would allow you to double the rebate on the wall insulation. Please call me to let me know if you are seriously considering adding insulation to your home, so that we can discuss this program in more detail. ****



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.



This grant can be accessed in combination with any Arctic Energy Alliance grant.

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

WINDOW UPGRADE



All of the windows in this house (12 total between the basement and main floor) are older double pane wood windows, that are in need of an upgrade. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $1100 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 30 GJ. 



There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, however there are rebates available from the Greener Homes Grant - Government of Canada. Please see their website for more details.



GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $125 or $250 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

</windows>
                <doors>

DOOR UPGRADE



The doors in your home are of various ages and styles, and replacement could be considered. Weather stripping was noted to be in need of replacement at all locations. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT for door replacements.



Savings: By replacing your existing doors with new ENERGY STAR certified doors, you could potentially save $ 90 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.3 GJ.



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $125 per hinged door system is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

NATURAL GAS Instantaneous Upgrade



You currently have a 151 litre natural gas fired hot water tank with an energy factor of 0.67. You may want to consider replacing your existing hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, could potentially save $250 per year in energy costs, slightly decrease your annual energy usage by 6.8 GJ,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program





</HotWaterSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has an older 13 liter toilet that should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush!



Savings: By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



NOTE: You have not yet applied to Greenerr Homes so the information in this report is for information purposes only and not applicable unless you apply for and are approved for participation in the Greener Homes Program by Natural Resources Canada (NRCan)



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



????????? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

????????? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

????????? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

????????? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

????????? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

????????? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Inuvik, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Not able to complete



An exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment, was unable to be performed in your home because both bathroom fans and the kitchen fan were not working at the time of the evaluation.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

       

        - two bathroom fans - both not working

        - a clothes dryer

      

You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Inuvik



The following December, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:



Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.73 per kWh

Oil - $1.73 per liter 

Natural Gas - $37.21 per GJ delivered

Wood - $341.25 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $14.70 per bag, $682.50 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Scott Dowler, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: scott.dowler@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!



